--TEST--
a contagious answer carries `tainted` through a union's member and through an array's element, so neither the plain `?string` nor the plain `array<string>` may be declared for it — `rule:security/unclassified-parameter-refuses-tainted`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The accepted half of this bound is
// tests/conformance/core/a-contagious-answer-carries-a-union-and-an-array.nvst,
// where `?tainted string` and `array<tainted string>` hold these same two
// answers and print. This file names the first laundering step in each shape,
// so a checker that qualified only the bare atom fails here while every case
// over a `string` answer still passes.
tainted string $t = "a,b";

// `rule:security/unclassified-parameter-refuses-tainted`: `Core\Str::after` returns `?string` and its subject is
// contagious. A nullable is a union, so the qualifier goes on the member that
// can carry it and `null` is left as it is -- declaring the plain union is the
// laundering this refuses.
?string $slice = Core\Str::after($t, ",");

// The same rule one container over. `Core\Str::split`'s `array<string>` takes
// the qualifier on its *element*, never on the array, because an array is not
// a qualifiable base.
array<string> $parts = Core\Str::split($t, ",");

// And the element still carries it after a subscript, which is what proves the
// contagion reached the element rather than the declaration having laundered
// on the way in.
array<tainted string> $kept = Core\Str::split($t, ",");
string $first = $kept[0];
echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `string|null`, found `null|tainted string`
   --> case.nvs:14:18
%A
error[E0401]: expected `array<string>`, found `array<tainted string>`
   --> case.nvs:19:24
%A
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `tainted string`
   --> case.nvs:25:17
%A
error: aborting due to 3 errors
